When [[Geography/Brithwyn/index|Brithwyn]] split from [[Geography/Calaria/index|Calaria]], it swore off the influence of outside organizations and chose instead to rule from within. The sole exception to that declaration is Protectorate.

A Brith [[Org/index|Org]] from the start, Protectorate is a private army-for-hire. Before the countries had split, Protectorate was _the_ company to hire when you had a government to destabilize or a war to wage. While the Calarian army was reserved for crusades and the like, Protectorate went to the highest bidder. When Brithwyn looked to break itself from its Calarian chains, Protectorate soldiers helped it free its bonds.

The Protectorate now generally acts as peacekeepers within Brithwyn, but they're still available for use in any conflict of one's choosing for the right price. The gems of the Protectorate army are their slayers: elite soldiers who've trained until they can enter a powerful "battletrance." Stories of entranced slayers singlehandedly tearing through rival encampments are familiar amongst mercenaries -- and it's believed that they're only slightly exaggerated.

A slayer enters the battle trance by biting into a small ampoule of liquid: the blood drains from their face, their eyes go bloodshot, and a red mist pours off them. In the trance they fight on past the end of a fight, and they do not always know friend from foe.

Aboard the [[Phenomena/Pilgrimage|pilgrimage]] the Protectorate would join the Party's alliance but would not merge its Heart or territory with the Party's, and it took over the berths left empty by the two companies that did.

The order's faith is its own. Its people hold that Aurelia I ascended and became one with the horizon, that the Aurelia the [[Org/Iridescent Church/index|Iridescent Church]] thrones in [[Geography/Calaria/index|Calaria]] is a false prophet, and that the true Aurelia is trapped far out on the horizon, unable to be reborn, singing for true believers to come and save her. To a Protectorate member the [[Phenomena/Harmony/Voidsong|Voidsong]] is very like that call, though they allow it may be some other singer and not her. Their fane aboard the pilgrimage is a tall cylinder with water moving through its inside for no purpose but its beauty, and the grass around it grows a deeper green than the rest of the strider's.
